o\ndocs/c.md:3: missing.md\ndocs/c.md:3: b.md#top\n```\n\nWait, line 3 has multiple links; the order of brokenness is regex findall order: a.md#c (valid), b.md (valid), missing.md (broken), b.md#top (broken). Both are on line 3. Output:\n\n```\ndocs/c.md:3: missing.md\ndocs/c.md:3: b.md#top\nexit: 1\n```\n\nThat's a good demo.\n\nShould I also test the slug logic with a heading that has punctuation? I could quickly add a test: a temp file with heading `## Hello, World!` → slug `hello-world`, and a link to `#hello-world` → valid. Let me do a quick sanity test in a scratch directory under the working directory (since /tmp is blocked).\n\nAlso test duplicate headings: `# A` twice → `a`, `a-1`. Quick test.\n\nNow let me write the new linkcheck.py. Keep it short.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Check local file links and #anchors in Markdown files under a directory.\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport re\nimport sys\n\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r\"\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)\")\nSCHEME_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:\")\nHEADING_RE = re.compile(r\"^#{1,6}\\s+(.*\\S)\\s*$\")\n\n\ndef slugify(text):\n    \"\"\"GitHub-style heading slug: links become text, lowercase, spaces to hyphens.\"\"\"\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\[([^\\]]*)\\]\\([^)]*\\)\", r\"\\1\", text)\n    text = re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s-]\", \"\", text).lower()\n    return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \"-\", text.strip())\n\n\ndef anchors_of(path):\n    \"\"\"Yield the GitHub-style slug of each heading in path (a, a-1, ...).\"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for line in f:\n            m = HEADING_RE.match(line)\n            if not m:\n                continue\n            slug = slugify(m.group(1))\n            n = counts.get(slug, 0)\n            counts[slug] = n + 1\n            yield slug if n == 0 else f\"{slug}-{n}\"\n\n\ndef local_target(raw):\n    \"\"\"Return (path, anchor) for a local link target, or None if not local.\"\"\"\n    target = raw.strip()\n    if target.startswith(\"<\") and target.endswith(\">\"):\n        target = target[1:-1].strip()\n    if SCHEME_RE.match(target) or target.startswith(\"#\"):\n        return None\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\"'):\n        target = parts[0]\n    path, _, anchor = target.partition(\"#\")\n    return (path, anchor) if path else None\n\n\ndef check_file(path):\n    \"\"\"Yield (line_number, target) for broken local links in path.\"\"\"\n    folder = os.path.dirname(path)\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            for raw in LINK_RE.findall(line):\n                target = local_target(raw)\n                if target is None:\n                    continue\n                file_part, anchor = target\n                full = os.path.join(folder, file_part)\n                if not os.path.exists(full):\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\n                elif anchor and anchor not in set(anchors_of(full)):\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\n\n\ndef main(argv):\n    directory = argv[1] if len(argv) > 1 else \".\"\n    broken = 0\n    for root, _dirs, files in os.walk(directory):\n        for name in sorted(files):\n            if not name.endswith(\".md\"):\n                continue\n            path = os.path.join(root, name)\n            for lineno, target in check_file(path):\n                print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target}\")\n                broken += 1\n    return 1 if broken else 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))\n```\n\nOne concern: `HEADING_RE` = `^#{1,6}\\s+(.*\\S)\\s*$` — a line like `# A` matches with group(1)=\"A\". A line `#NoSpace` doesn't match (GitHub requires a space). OK. Trailing closing hashes `## C ##` → group(1) = \"C ##\" → slugify strips `#` → \"c\". OK, matches GitHub.\n\nAlso, heading detection inside code blocks — keep it short, ignore.\n\n`anchors_of` is a generator; `anchor not in set(anchors_of(full))` materializes it — no problem for small files.\n\nNow update the sample files:\n\na.md:\n```\n# A\n\nStart here. Next: [b](b.md) or [c](c.md#c).\n```\n\nb.md unchanged:\n```\n# B\n\nBack to [a](a.md), or jump to [c](c.md).\n```\n\nc.md:\n```\n# C\n\nSee [a](a.md#c) and [b](b.md), plus [missing](missing.md) and [top](b.md#top).\n```\n\nRun `python3 linkcheck.py docs` → expected:\n```\ndocs/c.md:3: missing.md\ndocs/c.md:3: b.md#top\nexit: 1\n```\n\nAlso sanity-test slug edge cases in a scratch directory: heading `## Hello, World!` → `hello-world`; duplicate `# A` → `a`, `a-1`; link to `#hello-world` valid, `#a-1` valid, `#a-2` broken.\n\nLet me write it and run it.\n","seq":1736,"t":123071,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":5376,"draft_n":8540,"draft_n_accepted":3561,"predicted_ms":44020.0,"predicted_n":4781,"prompt_ms":610.0,"prompt_n":408},"to_request":625,"usage":{"cached_tokens":5376,"completion_tokens":4781,"prompt_tokens":5784}}
