{"items":[{"key":"seam-3/message-37","score":1,"sha256":"bb1615557218c261240e7a6b1e2ddbd483bd76d484f95c7cdc22aa0102e9b826"},{"key":"seam-3/message-32","score":1,"sha256":"e5d65dcd35052d0709dcd1bef10e29d3600216bd11e18208020ac6321b030df4"},{"key":"seam-3/message-22","score":1,"sha256":"2931c602310ff5822166db8dd27dba225bebc3abecb69c4a96bb4f5ee3032f64"}],"kind":"recalled","recall":"literal","seq":2808,"t":277943,"text":"<system-reminder>\n## Relevant memory\n\nUse the following memories only when they are directly relevant to the current request. Verify file/function claims before relying on them.\n\n### an answer (seam-3/message-37)\n\nImplemented. `linkcheck.py` now also validates `#anchor` parts of local links:\n\n- **`slugify()`** — GitHub-style slug: inline links in headings reduce to their text, punctuation (except `-` and `_`) is stripped, lowercased, spaces become hyphens. So `## Hello, World!` → `hello-world`.\n- **`anchors_of()`** — collects slugs of ATX headings (`#`–`######`), numbering duplicates GitHub-style: `a`, `a-1`, `a-2`.\n- **`local_target()`** — now returns `(path, anchor)`; external `scheme:` targets are still skipped, and a pure `#anchor` means \"same file\" (empty path). (First cut wrongly skipped same-file anchors — caught in testing.)\n- **`check_file()`** — if the file exists but the anchor isn't among its heading slugs, the link is reported. Output format (`file:line: target`) and exit codes are unchanged.\n\nSample updates: `a.md` links to `[c](c.md#c)` (valid anchor), `c.md` keeps `[missing](missing.md)` (broken file) and adds `[top](b.md#top)` (broken anchor — `b.md`'s only heading slugs to `b`).\n\nRun:\n```\n$ python3 linkcheck.py docs\ndocs/c.md:3: missing.md\ndocs/c.md:3: b.md#top\nexit: 1\n```\n\nVerified: duplicate-heading numbering (`#a-1` valid, `#a-2` flagged), punctuation slugs (`#hello-worl […]\n\n> NOTE: Relevant memory truncated for prompt budget.\n\n### a step that called tools (seam-3/message-32)\n\nbash {\"command\": \"python3 linkcheck.py docs; echo \\\"exit: $?\\\"\"}\nbash {\"command\": \"mkdir -p .slug && printf '# A\\\\n\\\\n# A\\\\n\\\\n## Hello, World!\\\\n\\\\n[ok1](#a) [ok2](#a-1) [ok3](#hello-world) [bad](#a-2)\\\\n' > .slug/t.md && python3 linkcheck.py .slug; echo \\\"exit: $?\\\"; rm -rf .slug\"}\n\n### a step that called tools (seam-3/message-22)\n\nTwo bugs surfaced: `a.md#c` should have been `a.md#a` (a.md's heading slug is `a`), and pure same-file anchors like `#a-2` were being treated as non-local and skipped. Fixing both:\n\n\n\n\nedit {\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"new_string\": \"if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\\n        return None\\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\\\"'):\\n        target = parts[0]\\n    path, _, anchor = target.partition(\\\"#\\\")\\n    return path, anchor\", \"old_string\": \"if SCHEME_RE.match(target) or target.startswith(\\\"#\\\"):\\n        return None\\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\\\"'):\\n        target = parts[0]\\n    path, _, anchor = target.partition(\\\"#\\\")\\n    return (path, anchor) if path else None\"}\nedit {\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"new_string\": \"file_part, anchor = target\\n                full = path if not file_part else os.path.join(folder, file_part)\", \"old_string\": \"file_part, anchor = target\\n                full = os.path.join(folder, file_part)\"}\nedit {\"file_path\": \"docs/c.md\", \"new_string\": \"See [a](a.md#a) and [b](b.md), plus [missing](missing.md) and [top](b.md#top).\", \"old_string\": \"See [a](a.md#c) a […]\n\n> NOTE: Relevant memory truncated for prompt budget.\n\n</system-reminder>","turn":4}
