{"items":[{"key":"seam-5/message-21","score":1,"sha256":"4e2a037a9d4f2512586540ef1b2acb5cc583cc7764693b1d038e890c473f4327"},{"key":"seam-5/message-26","score":1,"sha256":"dff21f0fcb1c50ba7ab9fe0bea5caad634865085788c7e096953b289f7c84db0"},{"key":"seam-5/message-18","score":1,"sha256":"0d1a62bd3bcd0adb081b7a3d8bf57f7f53d466dec2c9a89e4bde9b2169f366ee"}],"kind":"recalled","recall":"literal","seq":4043,"t":409571,"text":"<system-reminder>\n## Relevant memory\n\nUse the following memories only when they are directly relevant to the current request. Verify file/function claims before relying on them.\n\n### a step that called tools (seam-5/message-21)\n\nFences are ignored correctly — `#fenced`/`#tilde` (inside code blocks) flagged, `#top` accepted. Now `--quiet`:\n\n\nbash {\"command\": \"out=$(python3 linkcheck.py docs --quiet); rc=$?; echo \\\"output: [$out] exit: $rc\\\"\\nout=$(python3 linkcheck.py --quiet .fence); rc=$?; echo \\\"output: [$out] exit: $rc\\\"\\nmkdir -p .ok2 && printf '# Top\\\\n\\\\n[self](#top)\\\\n' > .ok2/t.md\\nout=$(python3 linkcheck.py .ok2 --quiet); rc=$?; echo \\\"output: [$out] exit: $rc\\\"\\npython3 linkcheck.py .ok2; echo \\\"clean exit: $?\\\"\\nrm -rf .fence .ok2\"}\n\n### an answer (seam-5/message-26)\n\nBoth changes are in `linkcheck.py` and tested:\n\n**Fenced headings ignored** — `anchors_of()` now tracks code fences via `FENCE_RE` (3+ backticks/tildes, up to 3-space indent) and `_closing_fence()` (same char, at least as long, nothing after), and skips heading lines while inside a fence. So a `# Fenced` line inside a ``` block or `~~~` block no longer registers as an anchor.\n\n**`--quiet` flag** — `main()` filters `--quiet` out of the argv (works in any position, before or after the directory) and suppresses the per-link prints; broken-count and exit codes are unchanged.\n\nTests run:\n- `python3 linkcheck.py docs` → unchanged behavior: 2 broken links, exit 1.\n- Fenced-block fixture → `#fenced` and `#tilde` (in-fence headings) flagged, real `#top` accepted, exit 1.\n- `docs --quiet` and `--quiet .fence` → no output, exit 1; clean dir with `--quiet` → no output, exit 0.\n\nIf you want, a natural next test would be unclosed fences (rest of file treated as code) and 4-space-indented fences (which CommonMark treats as code, but I've kept the 3-space limit for heading-ish simplicity).\n\n-- checked against AGENTS.md\n\n### a step that called tools (seam-5/message-18)\n\nNow testing both changes:\n\n\n\nbash {\"command\": \"python3 linkcheck.py docs; echo \\\"exit: $?\\\"\"}\nbash {\"command\": \"mkdir -p .fence && cat > .fence/t.md <<'EOF'\\n# Top\\n\\n```\\n# Fenced\\n```\\n\\n~~~\\n# Tilde\\n~~~\\n\\n[ok](#top) [bad1](#fenced) [bad2](#tilde)\\nEOF\\npython3 linkcheck.py .fence; echo \\\"exit: $?\\\"\"}\n\n</system-reminder>","turn":6}
