{"kind":"delta","request":4265,"seq":4580,"t":438197,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"new_string\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Check local file links in Markdown files under a directory.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport os\\nimport re\\nimport sys\\n\\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r\\\"\\\\[[^\\\\]]*\\\\]\\\\(([^)]+)\\\\)\\\")\\nSCHEME_RE = re.compile(r\\\"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:\\\")\\n\\n\\ndef local_target(raw):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return the local path for a link target, or None if not local (empty path = same file).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    target = raw.strip()\\n    if target.startswith(\\\"<\\\") and target.endswith(\\\">\\\"):\\n        target = target[1:-1].strip()\\n    if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\\n        return None\\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\\\"'):\\n        target = parts[0]\\n    return target.partition(\\\"#\\\")[0]\\n\\n\\ndef check_file(path):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yield (line_number, target) for broken local links in path.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    folder = os.path.dirname(path)\\n    with open(path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\\n            for raw in LINK_RE.findall(line):\\n                target = local_target(raw)\\n                if target is None:\\n                    continue\\n                full = path if not target else os.path.join(folder, target)\\n                if not os.path.exists(full):\\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\", \"old_string\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Check local file links and #anchors in Markdown files under a directory.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport os\\nimport re\\nimport sys\\n\\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r\\\"\\\\[[^\\\\]]*\\\\]\\\\(([^)]+)\\\\)\\\")\\nSCHEME_RE = re.compile(r\\\"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:\\\")\\nHEADING_RE = re.compile(r\\\"^#{1,6}\\\\s+(.*\\\\S)\\\\s*$\\\")\\nFENCE_RE = re.compile(r\\\"^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})\\\")\\n\\n\\ndef _closing_fence(line, fence):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"True if line closes the fence opened by `fence` (same char, at least as long).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    m = FENCE_RE.match(line)\\n    return bool(m) and m.group(1)[0] == fence[0] and len(m.group(1)) >= len(fence) and line[m.end():].strip() == \\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef slugify(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"GitHub-style heading slug: links become text, lowercase, spaces to hyphens.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    text = re.sub(r\\\"\\\\[([^\\\\]]*)\\\\]\\\\([^)]*\\\\)\\\", r\\\"\\\\1\\\", text)\\n    text = re.sub(r\\\"[^\\\\w\\\\s-]\\\", \\\"\\\", text).lower()\\n    return re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+\\\", \\\"-\\\", text.strip())\\n\\n\\ndef anchors_of(path):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yield the GitHub-style slug of each heading in path (a, a-1, ...).\\n\\n    Headings inside fenced code blocks are ignored.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    counts = {}\\n    fence = None\\n    with open(path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n        for line in f:\\n            if fence:\\n                if _closing_fence(line, fence):\\n                    fence = None\\n                continue\\n            m = FENCE_RE.match(line)\\n            if m:\\n                fence = m.group(1)\\n                continue\\n            m = HEADING_RE.match(line)\\n            if not m:\\n                continue\\n            slug = slugify(m.group(1))\\n            n = counts.get(slug, 0)\\n            counts[slug] = n + 1\\n            yield slug if n == 0 else f\\\"{slug}-{n}\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef local_target(raw):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return (path, anchor) for a local link target, or None if not local (empty path = same file).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    target = raw.strip()\\n    if target.startswith(\\\"<\\\") and target.endswith(\\\">\\\"):\\n        target = target[1:-1].strip()\\n    if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\\n        return None\\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\\\"'):\\n        target = parts[0]\\n    path, _, anchor = target.partition(\\\"#\\\")\\n    return path, anchor\\n\\n\\ndef check_file(path):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yield (line_number, target) for broken local links in path.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    folder = os.path.dirname(path)\\n    with open(path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\\n            for raw in LINK_RE.findall(line):\\n                target = local_target(raw)\\n                if target is None:\\n                    continue\\n                file_part, anchor = target\\n                full = path if not file_part else os.path.join(folder, file_part)\\n                if not os.path.exists(full):\\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\\n                elif anchor and anchor not in set(anchors_of(full)):\\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\"}","id":"call_2aca3ea764814f2fa51f3b29","index":0,"name":"edit"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I'll remove the anchor validation:\n\n1. Module docstring: \"Check local file links and #anchors in Markdown files under a directory.\" → \"Check local file links in Markdown files under a directory.\"\n2. Remove HEADING_RE, FENCE_RE, _closing_fence, slugify, anchors_of.\n3. `local_target`: simplify — anchors are no longer needed. Return the path (with title removed), or None if it's not local. Actually, if I'm only keeping file links, `local_target` should return the path or None. Rename it? Keep the name and change the docstring: \"Return the local path for a link target, or None if not local (empty path = same file).\" Should the returned path preserve the anchor part? No — anchors aren't checked at all, so `b.md#b` → file part is `b.md`. Therefore split at '#' and return the path part.\n\nNote: empty path = same file. `partition(\"#\")` — if the target is just `#anchor`, path becomes \"\" → same file, no problem.\n\n4. `check_file`: remove the anchor check.\n\nNew check_file:\n\n```python\ndef check_file(path):\n    \"\"\"Yield (line_number, target) for broken local links in path.\"\"\"\n    folder = os.path.dirname(path)\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            for raw in LINK_RE.findall(line):\n                target = local_target(raw)\n                if target is None:\n                    continue\n                full = path if not target else os.path.join(folder, target)\n                if not os.path.exists(full):\n                    yield lineno, raw.strip()\n```\n\nNew local_target:\n\n```python\ndef local_target(raw):\n    \"\"\"Return the local path for a link target, or None if not local (empty path = same file).\"\"\"\n    target = raw.strip()\n    if target.startswith(\"<\") and target.endswith(\">\"):\n        target = target[1:-1].strip()\n    if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\n        return None\n    parts = target.split(None, 1)\n    if len(parts) == 2 and parts[1].startswith('\"'):\n        target = parts[0]\n    return target.partition(\"#\")[0]\n```\n\nThis preserves handling of the title (the `\"B\"` part) and ignores the anchor.\n\nNote: AGENTS.md says Python files use 4-space indentation and a module docstring — already satisfied.\n\nThen a quick test: run against docs — the previous 2 broken links in docs — were those anchor links or file links? From earlier context: \"python3 linkcheck.py docs → 2 broken links, exit 1.\" I don't know whether those 2 were anchors or files. After removing anchor validation, the count might change (either drop to 0 or stay at 2). I'll run and report.\n\nAlso, the note about the working record:\n1. \"plan: No open work; optional follow-ups only if asked — verify title-with-anchor handling for `[x](b.md#b \"B\")` and decide whether headings inside fenced code blocks should be ignored.\" — no longer valid: since anchors are no longer checked at all, both follow-ups are moot. State that and mark as superseded.\n2. \"supersede: NONE -- checked against AGENTS.md\" — no longer valid: this step supersedes the earlier anchor decision. State that.\n3. \"Two small asks pending this turn...\" — already completed, but since the fence handling was removed, this entry no longer reflects the current state; mark as superseded (the fenced-heading skip no longer exists).\n\nShould I use update_record/resolve_entry? The system prompt mentions these tools as \"working object\" helpers. The notes say \"if no longer valid, say so; otherwise continue.\" I should state that explicitly in the reply, and resolve_entry for entries is also fine. I'll use resolve_entry for the entries that no longer hold. That seems appropriate. The first note is the \"plan\" entry — but resolve_entry takes the entry text. I'll call resolve_entry on the two that clearly no longer hold, and record the decision via update_record.\n\nActually, keep it concise: state that in the answer. The instructions say \"if no longer valid, say so; otherwise continue.\" I'll state it briefly and use resolve_entry/update_record to reflect it.\n\nAlright, let me do the edits.\n\nEdit 1: docstring.\nEdit 2: remove HEADING_RE, FENCE_RE, _closing_fence, slugify, anchors_of (one big block).\nEdit 3: local_target.\nEdit 4: check_file.\n\nLet me write the edits.\n","seq":4581,"t":438197,"text":"\n\nAnchor validation is being removed — the two follow-ups in that plan entry (title-with-anchor handling, fenced headings) become moot, and the \"supersede: NONE\" entry no longer holds since this step supersedes the earlier anchor decision. The \"two small asks\" entry is stale too: fence-skipping in `anchors_of` is now deleted entirely.\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":7936,"draft_n":3416,"draft_n_accepted":1724,"predicted_ms":18050.0,"predicted_n":2212,"prompt_ms":1450.0,"prompt_n":1644},"to_request":4265,"usage":{"cached_tokens":7936,"completion_tokens":2212,"prompt_tokens":9580}}
